Z||000000||SwampBat||05-20-99||08:57 PM||||OK, as I said in my other post I need to do the brakes, suspension, and I would like to beef up my 2.8   I thought aobut it and decided I am setting myself a $2000 budget to do everything I need to do to be able to spank  mudstains and all the little rice rockets in town.<br>About my car, its an 86 2m6 with 124000 miles<br>now If I can rebuild a sb chevy to pump out over 400hp, then I think I can do this for 2000 or less. I thought about putting a SB in the car but dont think I will, Im thinking about seeing what I can get out of the 2.8.<br>any comments or suggestions are greatly appreciated ......  Thanks for reading  SwampBat!
Z||000001||Shaun41178||05-20-99||09:54 PM||||Is this $2k budget just for the engien or for the whole car?  Doing all the wrok yourself or having someone else do all the labor?<p>Brakes will be around $500 depending on what you have replaced but new rotors cost around $50 each without labor.  If redoing all your brake componenets I suggest you upgrade and do the Grand Am brake upgrade.  All the parts will be cheaper and you will have better stopping as well.<p>Suspension will cost quite a bit too depending on what is needed.  new shcoks struts all around will run around $130 without labor.  Then an alighnment will be needed which will range from anywhere of $50 to $130  maybe you willneed new ball joints or tie rods too.  Do you need these things?<p>Do you plan on pulling the motor and having it rebuilt? or just upgrading with small bolt ons or a new cam?  With that many miles you probably have a good amount of blowby so a rebuild would be best.  this can run around $1k or more depending on how fancy you get.  Or try to find a OHV 3.4 pushrod to drop in.  You might be able to find one for around $600-$800 in a yard or look on the net.  The block will need some modifications which will cost a couple hundred more. <p>A tad bit more info is probably going to be needed from you so we can give more accurate info.
Z||000002||Pretender||05-20-99||10:19 PM||||the grand am up-grade?, are the grand-am's rotors and calipers larger and more efficient?
Z||000003||Carrolles||05-21-99||06:48 AM||||If you use the Grand Am brakes you will probably lose the parking brake.  This isn't much of an issue if you have an automatic trans or unless your state requires a working parking brake.
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||05-21-99||02:10 PM||||The info on swapping Grand Am brakes can be found here.  http://members.tripod.com/adm/popup/roadmap.shtml
